<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since the curator&rsquo;s last approval</h2>
<p>Four items, not yet read, oldest first. The curator read items 30 and 31 at 23:20 UTC on 7 November 2026, and no comment since marks a later item read. Item 34 landed on 8 November and stands as it was: the changes of 16 November do not touch its passages. Items 35 to 37 landed on 16 November, in two commits at 23:03 and 23:09 UTC, the second with the fixes after a cold read. Each shows the two commits as one change against the text before them. Drafts 38 to 40 take the next numbers, so the next landed change comes as item 41.</p>
<section id="l34" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 34 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;, and skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7</span>An item not yet read shows the current text, and a later change folds into it</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; underlined, the words that came in. No words of that text went.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;, line 204, the bullet on the page of skill drafts and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes: one sentence added at its end</p>
<ul><li>On the page of skill drafts and the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes, an item of a change that landed holds its new text whole, with the old words shown struck through and the new words marked, and its reason inside the item's Select range. The reason says in plain words what was wrong and what the text now says. It never cites a cold read's flag by its code or number, a judge's ruling by its number, or an item of a task by its number. The item has no collapsed current text.<span class="add"> An item that the curator has not said they read always shows the skill's current text: if a later change touches its passage, fold that change into the item, with its own reason beside the earlier one, in place of a new item.</span></li></ul>
<p class="where-in">references/skill-rewrite.md, &ldquo;The method&rdquo;, step 7, &ldquo;Landed changes&rdquo;, lines 24 to 27: four new bullets, after the bullet on every other item</p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add">A folded item is an item into which a later change was folded (<code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;). It strikes through the text before its first change, marks every word added since, and lists each change's reason in order.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">The curator's word that they read the page up to an item drops a folded item only if the fold was published before the word's time. Otherwise the item stays, with the fold marked.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">If a change touches the passages of two items that the curator has not said they read, fold it into each. Its part outside them gets its own item.</span></li>
<li><span class="add">The one item that a draft's landing fix gets is folded too, if its passage has an item that the curator has not said they read.</span></li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>A new rule.</strong> At 07:15 UTC on 8 November, on the page of <code>fortress-repo</code> changes, the curator asked: &ldquo;Why wasn&rsquo;t the page updated to reflect the current state of the skill?&rdquo; Change 39 there showed words that the skill no longer had, while change 51, further down the same page, had rewritten them. The coordinator had kept change 39 as it landed, so that the curator&rsquo;s open threads would stay on it, and had put the fix into a new item. The skill said what a landed item holds, but nothing of a later change to the same passage.</p>
<p>The rule now says: an item that the curator has not said they read always shows the skill&rsquo;s current text. A later change to its passage is folded into that item, with its own reason beside the earlier one, and does not become a new item. The rule stands once, in <code>SKILL.md</code>, &ldquo;Pages&rdquo;. Step 7 of the method gives the form of a folded item. It strikes through the text before its first change, marks every word added since, and lists the reason of each change in order. The curator&rsquo;s word that they read the page up to an item drops a folded item only if the fold was published before the word&rsquo;s time; otherwise the item stays, with the fold marked. A change that touches the passages of two items that the curator has not said they read is folded into each, and its part outside them gets an item of its own. And the one item that a draft&rsquo;s landing fix gets is folded too, if its passage has such an item.</p>
<p>A cold read then fixed the wording. The first commit stated the rule in both files. The second keeps it in <code>SKILL.md</code> alone, writes &ldquo;an item that the curator has not said they read&rdquo; in place of &ldquo;unread item&rdquo;, a term that the skill does not define, and puts the form of a folded item into step 7 in place of the repeated rule.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l35" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 35 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, the whole section</span>One boot: POSITIONS read whole, FACTS and INDEX searched, the full boot only at the curator&rsquo;s word</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, against the text before the change; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator. Steps 2 to 5 were steps 3 to 6 before the change, with the same words. Step 6 was step 7, and step 7 was step 2.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, lines 239 to 272</p>
<p>At session start and after each compaction, take these steps in order<span class="cut">:</span><span class="add">. If the curator names another form of the boot, that word replaces these steps. After a compaction, before step 0, load <code>fortress-repo</code>, then this skill, with the Skill tool. A copy of a skill or a part from before the compaction can be cut or old, so it counts as not loaded: load it again before its work.</span></p>
<ol start="0">
<li>Check the serving model: run <code>explorations/coordinator/tools/model-now.sh</code>, which prints the model of this turn's reply. If it prints nothing, use the <code>get_session</code> tool with no session ID: its <code>session_context.model</code> and <code>external_metadata.last_served_model</code>. If the model is neither Opus nor Fable, act as on the model check's alarm (below). Then check the transcript backup (below).</li>
<li><span class="cut"><code>FACTS.md</code>, </span><code>POSITIONS.md</code><span class="cut"> and <code>INDEX.md</code>, each</span><span class="add">,</span> whole. Read <span class="cut"><code>FACTS.md</code></span><span class="add">it</span> in pages: it <span class="cut">is larger than</span><span class="add">exceeds</span> one page of the Read tool.</li>
<li><code>PLAN.md</code>, its first section.</li>
<li>The boot note, then the held list.</li>
<li><code>git log --oneline -15</code>, for a worker's commit newer than the boot note.</li>
<li>If the boot note says that a batch runs, load <code>references/batch.md</code> and read what its "While it runs" names. Say nothing about the batch before that.</li>
<li>Load <code>references/delegating.md</code><span class="cut">, so that you give the work to workers and do not slip into doing it yourself</span><span class="add">, <code>references/record.md</code> and <code>fortress-repo</code>'s <code>references/records.md</code></span>.</li>
<li>Run <code>explorations/coordinator/check-index.sh</code><span class="add"> <code>| head -30</code>: it prints first the notes that lack an INDEX line</span>. Add a<span class="cut">n INDEX</span> line for each<span class="cut"> note that it reports</span><span class="add">, without reading INDEX whole</span>.</li>
<li>After a compaction, if the curator is not away ("Quiet times"), give a calibration check in a few short lines: your picture of the project's history, the current goals, where the work stands, and the next moves. It grounds you again in the work, and it shows your picture to the curator, who corrects it where it differs. Leave out a running batch.</li>
</ol>
<p><span class="cut"><strong>A light boot.</strong> When the curator asks for a light boot, take steps 0 and 3 to 8, and skip steps 1 and 2: FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX stay unread. Before you state a fact or a decision, or put anything to the curator, search the record with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>. The full boot, steps 0 to 8, is the default. The curator's word for a light boot replaces the hook's list of files after a compaction. After a light boot, do not read INDEX whole. Search it with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>. Then take these steps:</span></p>
<p><span class="add"><code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>INDEX.md</code> stay unread. Before you state a fact, or put anything to the curator, search them with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>. Never read INDEX whole. Read FACTS whole only at a batch landing:</span></p>
<ol>
<li>At the first batch landing after the boot, <span class="add">unless the boot was full, </span>read <code>FACTS.md</code> <span class="cut">and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> whole, <code>FACTS.md</code></span><span class="add">whole,</span> in pages. Do it before step 1 of <code>references/batch.md</code>, "The landing". If the <span class="cut">light </span>boot falls inside a batch landing, <span class="cut">after step 1 of "The landing", read them whole at once, before its next step. Naming the decisions that landed and planning the batch after it need both files whole.</span><span class="add">take the boot's steps, read FACTS whole, then go on with the landing. Ask the curator nothing about the boot.</span></li>
<li>When the record-landing workflow of step 4 of "The landing" ends, read what it changed in <span class="cut">the two files</span><span class="add">FACTS and POSITIONS</span>.</li>
<li>At a later batch landing <span class="cut">in the same session, with no compaction between</span><span class="add">before the next compaction</span>, read only what <span class="cut">the batch landing changed, as after a full boot</span><span class="add">it changed in the two files</span>.</li>
</ol>
<p><span class="add"><strong>The full boot.</strong> Only at the curator's word, read <code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>INDEX.md</code> whole too, after step 1. It costs about three times the default boot's context.</span></p>
<p><strong>The transcript backup.</strong> Check it <span class="cut">right after step 0 </span>at every boot<span class="cut">, a light boot included,</span> and after every VM restart, since the backup fails with no message:</p>
<ul>
<li>If the backup's worktree, <code>/home/user/fortress-transcripts-blinded</code>, is missing or not on its branch <code>transcripts-blinded</code>, re-arm the backup before the rest of the boot (the <code>cloud-container</code> skill, <code>references/container-loss.md</code>, "Re-arming the backup").</li>
<li>Otherwise, read the last line of its <code>.backup.log</code>. If the line says <code>push=failed</code>, or its time is older than the machine's last start (<code>uptime -s</code>), run <code>scripts/backup.sh --run</code> in the worktree by hand, and read the new last line. A sound line ends <code>push=ok</code>.</li>
<li>Re-arm only a worktree that is missing or broken. A snapshot, the backup's commit of the transcripts, older than the last restart is normal at the first boot after it: the backup runs only at a turn's end.</li>
</ul>
<p><span class="cut"><strong>At a landing.</strong> A batch landing's work never waits for a compaction or a boot, and you ask the curator nothing about the boot. After a full boot, read what the batch landing changed in the record, not FACTS and POSITIONS whole. After a light boot, follow the list under "A light boot".</span></p>
<ul>
<li>Read nothing that the boot does not name. List no directory, and bound every command's output.</li>
<li>To learn whether a worker runs, read the harness's notice at the top of the turn<span class="cut">, and <code>test -f</code> the one output path that its brief names</span>.<span class="add"> After an interrupt or a process stop, check each worker before you say what ran or was lost: an Agent-tool worker in that notice, a Workflow agent by its <code>result</code> line in the run's journal. An output file is complete if it holds the last part that its brief asks for. Relaunch a killed worker only as <code>references/delegating.md</code>, "Interrupts and process stops", says.</span></li>
<li>Rewrite the boot note whole at every change. Never append to it.</li>
<li>The model check runs at every turn's end, in every session that loads the repository's settings, headless sessions included. Its alarm makes the session take one more turn, with a message that starts <code>MODEL ALARM</code>. On the alarm:
<ul>
<li>make the alarm the first line of your reply, and send it as a push notification;</li>
<li>do no other work until step 0's check shows Opus or Fable again, or the curator says to go on.</li>
</ul>
<p>The alarm holds over the rules of "Quiet times", also over the "." reply at a check-in. A go on holds until the model changes again. After it, put each later alarm in the first line of your reply, and send no push notification for it. A message that starts <code>MODEL CHECK FAILED</code> means that the check could not read the model. Then check the model with <code>get_session</code>, and tell the curator in the first line of your reply. If the model is Opus or Fable, go on. If it is not, act as on the alarm.</p></li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>The default boot changed.</strong> The full boot was the default. It read FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX whole, which cost about 430K tokens of context. A light boot, at the curator&rsquo;s word, left all three unread. On the night of 16 November 2026, after a light boot, the coordinator stated things about the curator&rsquo;s decisions without having them in context.</p>
<p>The curator decided on 16 November that the default is the light boot with POSITIONS read whole. The section now says: at every boot the coordinator reads POSITIONS whole, and searches FACTS and INDEX with <code>facts-extract.sh</code> before it states a fact or puts anything to the curator. It never reads INDEX whole. It reads FACTS whole at the first batch landing after the boot, and after that only what a landing changed. The full boot, which reads FACTS and INDEX whole too, runs only at the curator&rsquo;s word. The paragraphs &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo; and &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; went, since the one boot holds their rules.</p>
<p>The boot now loads three parts as a fixed step, step 6: the part on delegation and the two parts on the record. A part that a boot step names is loaded. A part left to the moment of its work often was not.</p>
<p>Two smaller rules entered the section. After a compaction, a copy of a skill or of a part that the harness shows does not count as loaded, so the coordinator loads it again. One such copy was an older version that night. And after an interrupt or a process stop, the coordinator checks each worker, and checks that each output file is complete, not only that it exists. This is the curator&rsquo;s correction of 9 November 2026.</p>
<p>A cold read then fixed the wording, in the second commit: the two skills are loaded before step 0 after a compaction, the check of INDEX moved after the load of the parts with its output bounded, and a boot that falls inside a batch landing ends its steps before FACTS is read whole.</p></div>
</section>

